# PCO CLI

Use `pco` for Planning Center operations. Prefer intent-level commands for routine work and low-level ID commands for diagnosis or recovery.

Prefer read commands first:

```sh
pco me
pco blockouts list
pco plans list --count 10
pco plans show <plan-id>
pco plans items <plan-id>
pco songs search --query "<title>"
pco songs history --weeks 16
pco teams show <plan-id>
pco music team
pco music availability <YYYY-MM-DD>
pco music month <YYYY-MM>
```

Treat these as state-changing:

```sh
pco blockouts add <start-date> <end-date> --reason "<reason>"
pco blockouts delete <id>
pco plans create <YYYY-MM-DD> --template <template-id>
pco songs replace --date <YYYY-MM-DD> --current "<current title>" --with "<replacement title>"
pco songs create --title "<title>" --authors "<authors>" --arrangement-name "<name>" (--ccli <number> | --no-ccli)
pco songs set <plan-id> <item-id> <song-id>
pco songs add <plan-id> <after-item-id> <song-id> --label "<label>"
pco teams schedule <plan-id> <person-id> <team-id> "<position>"
pco teams unschedule <plan-id> <assign-id>
pco teams enable-signups <plan-id> --team-id <team-id>
```

For routine replacement, run `pco songs replace ... --dry-run --json`, then run it without `--dry-run` after the intended titles/date are confirmed. The command fails closed on ambiguous plans, items, songs, or arrangements, checks stale state, and verifies after mutation. Use `--plan-id`, `--arrangement-id`, or intentional `--song-only` when its error requests one.

`pco songs create` never adds a song to a plan. It requires title, authors, arrangement name, and explicit CCLI intent. Stop on duplicate matches unless the user explicitly authorizes `--allow-duplicate`. If arrangement creation partially fails, never delete the created song; use the returned `resume_command` with `--resume-song-id`.

Keep theological judgment, musical selection, service-flow policy, and repetition policy outside the CLI.

Before mutating:

- Confirm IDs, dates, and positions.
- Check availability before scheduling.
- Never invent person IDs, plan IDs, item IDs, song IDs, team IDs, or positions.
- If the user did not explicitly request the exact mutation, propose the command and wait.

After mutating, re-run the relevant read command and summarize what changed.
